title: A second specialist joins a plan as the answerer of one question, the builder of its own part, or, where the hardest decision needs what two territories know, a proposer who writes before reading the other; never as a second author.
pid: 436d5e47-89df-41ab-b763-5f989994883e
body: |2-
  For Top at step 2 of [[id:6b88547d-78a8-4a64-a7e7-b754b201d97a][the planning procedure]], and for each plan's author.
  Written 2026-11-13 for [[id:fe818784-e776-42da-a948-e7bcd14e86d7][Top's mission]], after Jeff:
  "It seems powerful, but also potentially messy, to have different
  specialists work on different aspects of the same plan."
  The rule, for each planned request:
  - One specialist authors the plan, and it alone decides.
  - A specialist whose territory the plan changes builds its own part,
    after the parts it depends on, never beside them. The plan gives a
    whole example of what passes between two parts before either is built.
  - A specialist that knows a fact the author needs answers one
    question: the author writes it in its 'to Claude', Top sends a
    short mission, the answer goes in the answerer's graph. A builder
    whose part comes first may answer in its building mission, as Drew did.
  - When the hardest decision needs what two territories know and
    neither map says, so that the author cannot name the question (a
    border inside one function, like Val's and Terry's), its two owners
    each write a proposal for that decision in their own graphs,
    leading with what only their territory shows; the second writes
    without reading the first. The author, one of the two, then reads
    both and decides, recording the other's proposal as an alternative
    taken or rejected, and why. One round; nobody answers the answer.
  - Never: two authors in turns, a debate between specialists, two
    builders at once on parts that share a contract, or a reader who
    turns author.
  Inside one mission a builder may give separable pieces to subagents
  at once, if the words they share are fixed before they start. Vic
  did so for the renaming in his plan's part 2, and the subagents
  agreed on every shared string without seeing each other.
  The sources are in [[id:b034bd82-6ed1-48cb-81d5-d492db9209b3][what I read]], under "on working together";
  each claim below says whether I read it there, inferred it, or did not check it.
